<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    Learning something and still having it in September are different
    achievements, and school mostly measures the first. Everyone has
    experienced the second failing: a subject you genuinely understood once,
    gone.
  </p>
  <p>
    Forgetting is lawful rather than random. It happens on a curve, and a
    check placed just as something is about to fade resets that curve for
    much longer than the check cost.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">How it works</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Widening intervals") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Something learned today comes back in a few days, then a couple of
        weeks, then months — each time you get it, the next gap grows. Get
        it wrong and it comes back sooner.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Scheduled per topic, per person") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The schedule is computed from your own history with that specific
        topic, using an algorithm built for exactly this. Nobody reviews on
        a generic timetable.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Short on purpose") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        A handful of questions, a few minutes, on the days there's something
        due. A review queue you dread is a review queue that stops
        happening.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Retrieval, not rereading") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        You're asked to produce the answer, not to look at it again.
        Rereading feels like learning and mostly isn't; being asked is
        uncomfortable and works.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Where the questions come from</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    From what you've actually been learning — the topics on your map, and
    the notes you wrote. Any note in the app can become a quick check on the
    spot, which is a fast way to discover that having read something is not
    the same as knowing it.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Why this belongs in a life assistant</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Because the review that fits into a life is the one that survives.
    Sitting inside the app that already knows your week means the day's
    handful of questions appears beside everything else you're doing, rather
    than in a separate app you'd have to remember to open — which is how
    every good intention about revision has historically ended.
  </p>
</section>
